# FIVE COMPUTERS, THREE DISPLAYS, AND AN ASCENT -- the narrated version of
# 4gpc-ops1-plus-gpc5.script, for recording.  Run from discretePanel/:
#
#   python3 simulatePASS.py --gpcs 1-5 --crts 3 --size 384 --tape ~/workspace/pass-run/OI340700-v44boot.mmv --layout examples/5gpc-3crt-subtitled.layout --script examples/5gpc-3crt-subtitled.script --duration 2400
#
# --layout is what starts subtitles.py; without it the captions below go
# nowhere and the run is simply the unnarrated one.
#
# --size 384 IS NOT OPTIONAL, though --scale is: scale already defaults to
# 0.8, but size defaults to 512, and the layout beside this file records
# window positions taken at 384.  Ask for 512 and the windows will not sit
# where the layout puts them.
#
# WHAT IS BEING SHOWN.  The orbiter's real ascent arrangement: four General
# Purpose Computers flying as ONE redundant set, executing the same software
# on the same inputs in lockstep, and a fifth computer that is NOT part of it
# and drives its own display.  On the vehicle that fifth machine runs the
# Backup Flight System; we have no BFS, so it stays in OPS 0 -- still a fifth
# computer on every bus, still in the common set, visibly running something
# else.
#
# THE THREE THINGS A VIEWER SHOULD COME AWAY WITH, and the script is paced to
# make each visible rather than merely true:
#
#   1. Each computer is loaded from a tape, one at a time, by hand.  There is
#      no operating system until somebody flips switches.
#   2. Ascent cannot be commanded from a cold machine.  OPS 0 -> OPS 901 ->
#      OPS 101 is the vehicle's own sequence and the flight software enforces
#      it: DM6OPS's transition table simply does not list 2101 as reachable
#      from OPS 0.  The script goes through G9 because it must.
#   3. A display belongs to whichever computer the crew gives it to, and a
#      GPC runs up to three major functions at once -- so the same computer
#      can show an ascent trajectory on one screen and a memory display on
#      another, which is exactly what CRT3 demonstrates at the end.
#
# EVERY WAIT IN HERE IS A CONDITION, NOT A GUESS.  The IPLs are gated on the
# GPCIPL menu appearing and on the MODE talkback reading RUN, so a slow host
# stretches the recording without ever desynchronising the captions from what
# is on screen.  The only fixed delays are between a caption and the action
# it describes, and the reading time of a caption that follows one.
#
# THE TWO OPS LOADS ARE GATED ON WHAT THE DISPLAY SAYS, and on the right
# thing -- see the note beside the 9011 wait for the version that was gated
# on the wrong one and cost a recording.  G9 is waited for by its major mode,
# because its display shares the name GPC MEMORY with OPS 0; ascent is waited
# for by the format announcing itself as LAUNCH TRAJ.
#
# RUN, WATCHED AND RECORDED, 2026-09-23.  It comes up: CRT1 and CRT2 both on
# 1011 LAUNCH TRAJ 1 under GPC1 and GPC2, four computers flying an ascent as
# one redundant set, and CRT3 on 0001 GPC MEMORY under GPC5 -- the fifth
# machine outside the set, on its own display.

# ONE SECOND BETWEEN KEYS, not the 0.35 a measurement uses.  An ITEM entry
# typed briskly is a blur to somebody being shown what an ITEM entry IS; at a
# second apiece each key is seen to land.  It governs the IPL script this one
# calls as well, so the ITEM 1 EXEC in each of the five IPLs slows too.
keygap 1

+0     subtitle This is a demonstration in which we run Space Shuttle\nflight software version OI-34.07, as used for flight STS-134.
+5     subtitle But first, a brief tour of the visuals.
+5     subtitle <left>Commander's (CDR) display (CRT1) and keyboard.\nComputer Annunciation Matrix (CAM): for\nindicating out-of-sync computers.
+6     subtitle <right>Pilot's (PLT) display (CRT2) and keyboard.\nManager: a supervisor for various\nfunctions of the simulator itself.
+6     subtitle Panel: a collection of controls from various\nlocations on the full control panel.  CRT3, a\ndisplay shared by the CDR and the PLT.
+5     subtitle

+1     subtitle First, power up all five computers.\n(Look at the upper left of the Panel.)
+5 gpc 1
+0 power on
+1 gpc 2
+0 power on
+1 gpc 3
+0 power on
+1 gpc 4
+0 power on
+1 gpc 5
+0 power on

# --- all five GPCs to HALT, the three IDPs on --------------------------------
+1     subtitle Next, put all five computers into the HALT state.\n(Look at the bottom left of the Panel.  HALT is the\nsim's default, so they may be in HALT already.)
+5   gpc 1
+0   mode HALT
+0   gpc 2
+1   mode HALT
+0   gpc 3
+1   mode HALT
+0   gpc 4
+1   mode HALT
+0   gpc 5
+1   mode HALT

+1   subtitle Apply power to displays 1-3 and their\nIntegrated Display Processors (IDP).\n(Look at the Panel's upper right.)
+5   idppower 1 on
+1   idppower 2 on
+1   idppower 3 on
+1   subtitle Set Major Function (MF) to GNC for each\nof displays 1-3, if not already set that way.\n(Look at the Panel's upper right corner.)
+5   majfunc 1 gnc
+1   majfunc 2 gnc
+1   majfunc 3 gnc

# --- the four that will form the redundant set -------------------------------
+1   subtitle We're going to load the software for each of\nthe five computers from one of the two Mass\nMemory Units (MMU), or "tape drives".
+5   subtitle To load the first computer (GPC1), we'll use\nthe CDR display (CRT1) and keyboard (KYBD1).
+5   script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1
+2   subtitle In contrast to GPC1, to load GPC2-GPC4 we'll use\nthe PLT display (CRT2) and keyboard (KYBD2).
#+4   subtitle First, deassign CRT2 from it previous driver.
#+4   keys KB1 GPC/CRT 0 2 EXEC
+1   script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
#+2   subtitle Deassign CRT2 from its previous driver.
#+4   keys KB1 GPC/CRT 0 2 EXEC
+2   script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=3 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
#+2   subtitle Deassign CRT2 from its previous driver.
#+4   keys KB1 GPC/CRT 0 2 EXEC
+2   script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=4 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2

# --- then GPC5, onto CRT3, which is also how IDP3 gets its background --------

+2   subtitle Computers GPC1-GPC4 are synchronized,\nrunning identical Primary Flight Software\n(PFS), and forming a "redundant set".
+5   subtitle GPC5 is different.  It doesn't syncronize with\nGPC1-4, and instead runs the Backup Flight\nSoftware (BFS).  So the procedure differs.
+5   subtitle (We don't have a copy of BFS, so we're\njust going to load a different portion of\nPFS and pretend that it's BFS.)

+2   subtitle And now we load GPC5. Recall that GPC1-4 are redundant/synchronized.\nGPC2 won't be synchronized with the others, because it's for running BFS.\nWe'll watch that on CRT3, the display shared by the CDR and PLT.
+5   subtitle For loading GPC5, we'll use the shared center\ndisplay (CRT3).  There's no dedicated keyboard,\nbut we can borrow the PLT keyboard (KYBD2).
+5   subtitle In the middle right of the Panel, notice\nthe RIGHT IDP/CRT SEL switch.  We're\ngoing to flip it from CRT 2 to CRT 3.
+5   kybdsel right 3
+2   subtitle Notice that the yellow line at the bottom of CRT2\nmoves instead to the bottom of CRT3.  That\nline points to the keyboard used by the CRT.
+5   script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=5 crt=3 idp=3 kb=KB2
+2   subtitle CRT3 doesn't need a keyboard any more, so\nreturn the RIGHT IDP/CRT SEL switch to CRT 2.
+5   kybdsel right 2

+2   subtitle All five computers are now loaded and running\nOperational Sequence (OPS) 000, otherwise\nknown as the System Software (SSW).
+5   subtitle Now it gets harder.

# --- take CRT3 out of both NBATs --------------------------------------------
+5   subtitle The Shuttle has a variety of dedicated "databuses"\nthat interconnect the computers and peripherals\nin lots of ways.  These need to be configured.

+5   subtitle The original documentation lists various databus\nconfigurations used in different flight phases.\nWe'll set up an Ascent/Entry configuration
+5   subtitle We'll use the CDR keyboard (KYBD1) and display (CRT1).
+5   subtitle As we go through the configuration\nsteps, changes can be seen in the\nupper left of display CRT1 (or CRT2).
+5   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 + 9 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 + 9 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 + 1 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 + 1 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 2 + 1 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 2 + 1 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 3 + 2 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 3 + 2 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 4 + 3 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 4 + 3 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 5 + 4 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 5 + 4 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 4 + 1 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 7 + 1 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 8 + 2 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 8 + 2 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 9 + 3 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 9 + 3 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 0 + 4 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 0 + 4 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 1 + 0 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 1 + 0 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 2 + 1 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 2 + 1 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 3 + 2 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 3 + 2 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 8 + 1 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 8 + 1 EXEC
+1   subtitle <left>Use keys ITEM 1 9 + 2 EXEC
+2   keys ITEM 1 9 + 2 EXEC

+3   subtitle Move CRT3 off of Major Function GNC, so\nthat the Ascent software won't claim it.\n(Look in the upper right of the Panel.)
+5   majfunc 3 pl

# --- OPS 0 -> OPS 901 -> OPS 101 --------------------------------------------
+2   subtitle We want to put our Redundant Set (RS) of\nGPCs into Ascent mode (OPS 1).  But that\ncan't be done directly from OPS 000.
+5   subtitle First we have to go from OPS 0 to OPS 9, and then to OPS 1.

+5   subtitle <left>Use keys OPS 9 0 1 PRO
+2   keys OPS 9 0 1 PRO
+1   subtitle GPC1-4 are now loading Memory Configuration\n(MC) 9 together.  That's OPS 9.  (Look for the red\nACTIVITY indicator at the bottom of the PANEL.)

# WAIT FOR THE MAJOR MODE, WHICH IS THE ONLY THING THAT CHANGES.  Both of
# the obvious conditions are met INSTANTLY and are therefore no wait at all:
#
#   'new-screen' is any page change, and the page changes at once
#   'title GPC MEMORY' is what OPS 0 ALREADY shows
#
# G9's display is called GPC MEMORY too -- the difference is the major mode
# in the corner, 0001 before and 9011 after.  Measured the hard way on
# 2026-09-23: with 'new-screen' the wait was met after 0.1 s, OPS 101 was
# keyed ELEVEN seconds after OPS 901 instead of the ninety the unnarrated
# script allows, the ascent request went into a machine still loading G9 and
# was lost, and the run sat at the LAUNCH TRAJ wait until it timed out with
# CRT1 reading "9011/ / GPC MEMORY 1".
wait crt 1 title "9011" timeout 120

+1   subtitle The RS is now in OPS 9, and specifically in menu\nOPS 901.  (Look in the upper left corner of CRT1\nor CRT2.)  But we need to go to menu OPS 101.
+5   subtitle <left>Use keys OPS 1 0 1 PRO
+2   keys OPS 1 0 1 PRO
+1   subtitle The entire Redundant Set (RS) is now loading\nOPS 1 in lockstep.  Observe CRT1, CRT2, and\nthe red ACTIVITY indicator at Panel bottom.

# The ascent display announces itself by name, so wait for the name.
wait crt 1 title LAUNCH TRAJ timeout 120

# --- and finally, give CRT3 to GPC5 -----------------------------------------
+0   subtitle We're now in OPS 1, as you can tell by the\n"1011" in the displays' upper left corners or\nby the menus' titles "LAUNCH TRAJ 1".

+0     audio /usr/share/sounds/freedesktop/stereo/complete.oga

#+5   subtitle But look at CRT3:  It's titled "GPC MEMORY" and\nis also driven by GPC1.  How can CRT1 and CRT3\nsimultaneously be driven differently by GPC1?
#+5   subtitle Each GPC can simultaneously drive displays\in three different Major Functions (MF):  GNC,\nSM, and PL.  (Look at Panel upper right.)
#+5   subtitle So ... mystery solved!  Nevertheless, we do want\nCRT3 to be driven by GPC5 rather than GPC1.\nFirst, give CRT3 the PLT keyboard again.
#+5   kybdsel right 3
#+2   subtitle <right>Use the keys GPC/CRT 5 3 EXEC
#+2   keys KB2 GPC/CRT 5 3 EXEC
#+1   subtitle Give the PLT keyboard back to the PLT display.
#+2   kybdsel right 2

+3   subtitle Hopefully you'll now see that CRT1 is driven by GPC1, CRT2 is driven by GPC2,\nand CRT3 is driven by GPC5.  How can you tell?  Look at the clock at the top right\nof the display.  The number just to the left of the clock is the GPC driving the CRT.
+5   subtitle In summary, four computers flying an Ascent as one computer,\nand a fifth along-side them, outside the set, on its own display.
+5   subtitle Our little demo is now concluded.
+5   subtitle

# AND IT IS OVER.  A script that ends in silence ends while the person
# recording it is looking somewhere else.  Any file will do; this one is a
# sound the desktop already ships, and the name is checked when the script
# is checked.
+0     audio /usr/share/sounds/freedesktop/stereo/complete.oga
